  "metadata": {
    "title": "SpaceX IPO: Nasdaq is the home for 'the trillion dollar companies'",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "00:00 Speaker A\nNelson, how important is it the relationship, relationship aspect to all of this? This is the second, uh, really Elon Musk company that Nasdaq has brought public. And I remember Tesla as if it was yesterday, but it was, I mean it was years ago, at this point. Um, why does Elon trust you guys the most?\n00:23 Nelson\nNasdaq is home to the trillion dollar companies. So you look at the companies that are driving the innovation economy, they do list on Nasdaq. We have had a good relationship with them and their team. If you think about how companies evaluate an IPO, they do think about brand, they think about areas such as how do they help us communicate with investors, index opportunities. And then it's the the partnership. So over the years, things come up as a public company and they want to know they can come to Nasdaq for advice on on regulatory matters, on market matters, and even when you just think about the overall business ecosystem that comes on Nasdaq, how we connect them to our community. So, I think trust is everything and I I have been here 25 years, so it has meant a lot to us and our partner companies to to have those long-term relationships.\n01:25 Speaker A\nThis is a hell of a thing to have happened, Nelson. You have the world's largest IPO and by and large, knock on wood, it's going to go off relatively seamlessly, at least to us watching this uh from afar. We're only a couple miles away from you. Um, do you see yourself as in the pole position to land OpenAI and Anthropic?\n01:52 Nelson\nWell, we we never take any anything for granted uh at all. And when we get to deals, we we focus at Nasdaq as a company on on most every IPO, but you get to companies of this size. This is what we call a one-Nazic effort. Obviously the very, very uh important companies, generational companies, so we're highly focused on them. And I do think when you look at where the largest, most innovative companies in the world list, today it's on Nasdaq. But we don't take that for granted. We're going to work very hard to show them that we are are the best home for them.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "00:00 Speaker A",
      "Nelson, how important is it the relationship, relationship aspect to all of this? This is the second, uh, really Elon Musk company that Nasdaq has brought public. And I remember Tesla as if it was yesterday, but it was, I mean it was years ago, at this point. Um, why does Elon trust you guys the most?",
      "00:23 Nelson",
      "Nasdaq is home to the trillion dollar companies. So you look at the companies that are driving the innovation economy, they do list on Nasdaq. We have had a good relationship with them and their team. If you think about how companies evaluate an IPO, they do think about brand, they think about areas such as how do they help us communicate with investors, index opportunities. And then it's the the partnership. So over the years, things come up as a public company and they want to know they can come to Nasdaq for advice on on regulatory matters, on market matters, and even when you just think about the overall business ecosystem that comes on Nasdaq, how we connect them to our community. So, I think trust is everything and I I have been here 25 years, so it has meant a lot to us and our partner companies to to have those long-term relationships.",
      "01:25 Speaker A",
      "This is a hell of a thing to have happened, Nelson. You have the world's largest IPO and by and large, knock on wood, it's going to go off relatively seamlessly, at least to us watching this uh from afar. We're only a couple miles away from you. Um, do you see yourself as in the pole position to land OpenAI and Anthropic?",
      "01:52 Nelson",
      "Well, we we never take any anything for granted uh at all. And when we get to deals, we we focus at Nasdaq as a company on on most every IPO, but you get to companies of this size. This is what we call a one-Nazic effort. Obviously the very, very uh important companies, generational companies, so we're highly focused on them. And I do think when you look at where the largest, most innovative companies in the world list, today it's on Nasdaq. But we don't take that for granted. We're going to work very hard to show them that we are are the best home for them."
    ],
